## EC-05 | Formulary Updated Exactly at Midnight — Timestamp Boundary

| Field | Detail |
|-------|--------|
| **Test ID** | EC-05 |
| **Scenario** | The nightly batch job completes at exactly 00:00:00. A member on HMO Classic searches at 00:00:01 — one second after the cache refresh. The system must display the correct timestamp: the new batch run time, not the previous night's |
| **Why Risky** | Timestamp boundary bugs are classic off-by-one errors in batch processing systems. If the timestamp displayed is the previous run (23:59 the prior evening) instead of 00:00 of the current day, the member sees data that appears to be nearly 24 hours old when it is actually 1 second old. For compliance, the timestamp must accurately reflect data currency — Sandra's email flagged this as an audit risk |
| **Expected Behavior** | Timestamp displayed reflects the actual completion time of the most recent batch run — updated within seconds of the batch completing. No hardcoded "yesterday 11:59 PM" artifact. Timezone is explicit (e.g., "Last updated: Apr 27, 2026 at 12:00 AM ET") |
| **Likelihood** | 🟡 Medium |
| **Impact** | 🔴 High |

