## EC-10 | Drug Covered Under One Plan Tier But Member Recently Hit Deductible

| Field | Detail |
|-------|--------|
| **Test ID** | EC-10 |
| **Scenario** | The formulary shows a drug as Tier 2 with a standard copay. However, the member has not yet met their deductible for the plan year — meaning they will actually pay full cost, not the Tier 2 copay. The pricing API (which would know deductible status) is out of scope for Sprint 14 |
| **Why Risky** | This is the exact scenario Sandra flagged in her compliance email — the ACA disclaimer exists precisely because of this gap. However, the risk is that if the disclaimer is present but visually de-emphasized (small font, grey text, below the fold), members will focus on the tier label and ignore the disclaimer entirely. The member walks to the pharmacy expecting a $15 Tier 2 copay and pays $180 because their deductible hasn't been met. The compliance risk is real even when the disclaimer technically exists |
| **Expected Behavior** | ACA disclaimer must be: (a) visually prominent — not buried below results, (b) rendered before or alongside the tier/copay indicator — not after, (c) readable at all standard zoom levels including 200% zoom for accessibility. This edge case validates not just that the disclaimer exists (covered in functional tests) but that its placement and prominence actually serve the member's understanding |
| **Likelihood** | 🔴 High — near-universal for members early in the plan year |
| **Impact** | 🔴 High |

